92fcd4f5fb7f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186527262 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186527163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537084 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537115 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537084 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537084 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $x^{2} + y^{2}=16.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(-4,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537084 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "00382f0d-ea9b-5110-b24f-8be62b8690a5" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537084 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set $f(x,y)=x^{2} + y^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=16.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(-4,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(-4,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(-4,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2 x)\\,i+(2 y)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(-4,0) & =(2 (-4))\\,i+(2 (0))\\,j \\\\  & =(-8)\\,i+(0)\\,j \\\\  & =⟨-8,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(-4,0)=⟨-8,0⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(-4,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-{x}_{0}){v}_{y}-(y-{y}_{0}){v}_{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-(-4))(0)-(y-(0))(-8) & =0 \\\\ 8 y & =0 \\\\ y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537092 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $3 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=7.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,-1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3c33c871-3aa8-55d7-b3c4-b164edfa69d7" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186537098 #edb/ref 1074 "If we set 