186045417] [17592186544775 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186544586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544775 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544775 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544775 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186544284 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544775 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f4dfd42d-e350-5855-be90-2f0889abb592" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544794 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544794 #edb/ref 1069 "q-96576" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544794 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544794 #edb/ref 1071 "The discrete random variable $X\\in Z$ can be approximated by the continuous random variable $Y.$ By applying an appropriate continuity correction, $P(X=-18)$ can be approximated as" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544794 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e025671-c988-5444-b52c-2cc6ba70eb20" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544794 #edb/ref 1074 "We construct an interval around $X=-18$ with a radius of $0.5:$\n\n![](images/9c/9ccbf2335f4db52e60760d4740ebf29ac054b6b249b6f0436e02fbff3311d7d4.png)\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=-18) & ≈P(-18-0.5\\le Y\\le -18+0.5) \\\\  & =P(-18.5\\le Y\\le -17.5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1069 "q-130722" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the plane curve $\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(1,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4e15364c-186a-5328-8307-13438fe58309" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544815 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y) & ={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=-1.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(1,0)$ can be found by computing $∇f(1,0).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(1,0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}y)-{x}^{2}+{y}^{2})j \\\\  & =(2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)-2x)i+({x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}y)+2y)j \\\\ ∇f(1,0) & =(2(1)(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))-2(1))i+((1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(({1}^{2})(0))+2(0))j \\\\  & =-2\\,i+j \\\\  & =⟨-2,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(1,0)=⟨-2,1⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(1,0).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-1}{-2} & =\\frac{y-0}{1} \\\\ x-1 & =-2y \\\\ x+2y-1 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544818 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544765 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544818 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132566" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544818 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544818 #edb/ref 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following double integrals, express the following sum as a single double integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544818 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5502822c-28cd-5be6-838d-dcd2d5622d7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544818 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/31/314b881b95ea804456b3e41a1d8fec70cf459e007e2120d9018e3bef02f1b523.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the region of integration. It consists of two type II regions, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,1\\le x\\le 3\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1,\\,{e}^{y-1}\\le x\\le 3\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $x=x(y),$ is\n\n$$\nx={e}^{y-1}\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1,\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration, we write $D$ as a type I region. The type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/c9/c9b097164e2b5215a051e18de055f3cce4c26600caf510bc3b0071b77b645089.png)\n\nTherefore, swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y+{\\int }_{1}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3+1}{\\int }_{{e}^{y-1}}^{3}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186544709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-132552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544889 #edb/ref 1071 "On a busy waterway, barges pass by a particular point continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate of one every $10$ minutes. Determine the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one. Round your answer to four decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e3898571-1060-5a54-af07-b5592b492d4f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544889 #edb/ref 1074 "Since barges pass continuously, independently, and at a constant average rate, we can model the time between two successive barges using an exponential distribution.\n\nAn exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ be the amount of time between two successive barges. Since a barge passes every $10$ minutes, the rate is $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10}$ barges per minute.\n\nThe time interval between two successive barges can be modeled as an exponential random variable with rate $\\lambda =\\frac{1}{10},$ and we can write \n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(\\frac{1}{10}).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that the next barge will pass between $5$ and $7$ minutes after the previous one is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(5<X<7) & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{5}^{7}\\frac{1}{10}{e}^{-x/10}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x/10}{|}_{5}^{7} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-7/10}+{e}^{-5/10} \\\\  & ≈0.1099\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544902 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0cfd11fc-dd9a-5b3c-8a96-28e4560b2041" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544902 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544906 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "19c28e52-fdaf-5ad1-b61f-5be844edc12c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544906 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186268879 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544910 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2900fd92-b1ba-5748-8fdb-cafa51cae4b8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544910 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544889 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544912 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186544968 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544912 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "29d93015-c02a-5f8e-8294-085ca82c8b5d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186544912 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186544818 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
